   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the executive law and the labor law, in relation to the\npublication of information regarding waivers of compliance with state contract\nprovisions concerning minority and women-owned business enterprise\nparticipation requirements",
    "basePrintNo": "S4165",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S4165-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§313 & 311, Exec L; amd §21, Lab L",
    "lawSection": "Executive Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S4165\n\nSPONSOR: BAILEY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the executive law and the labor law, in relation to the\npublication of information regarding waivers of compliance with state\ncontract provisions concerning minority and women-owned business enter-\nprise participation requirements\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to allow the public to see which contracts\nhave been waived.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1:(a) if it appears that a contractor cannot comply with the\nMinority and women-owned business enterprise participation requirements\nset forth in a particular contract, the contractor may file a written\napplication with the contracting agency requesting a partial or total\nwaiver of such requirements, setting forth their inability to meet any\nrequirements with an explanation of the efforts the contractor has\nundertaken.\n\n(b) Within 30 days of the issuance of the waiver of compliance the\ncontracting agency shall report the waiver to the department of labor\nand to the director of the division of minority and women's business\ndevelopment in the department of economic development and to publish the\ninformation\n\nSection 2: To file a complaint where the director has knowledge that a\nContractor may have violated the provisions that were unrelated, sepa-\nrate or distinct from the state contract as expressed by its terms and\nto streamline the state certification process to accept federal and\nmunicipal corporation certifications and to keep record of partial and\ntotal waivers issued\n\nSection 2: Where section 21 of the labor law is amended stating that\nthey shall compile on an annual basis, a list of waivers of compliance\nwith state contract provisions concerning minority and women-owned busi-\nness enterprise participation requirements\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIn order to support the participation goals for minority and women-owned\nbusiness enterprises (MBEs) throughout New York State, this bill aims to\nensure that a good faith effort has been made to comply participation\nrequirements set forth in a particular state contract in order to\nreceive partial or total waivers in the event that there is not reason-\nable availability of MWBE contractors, and that the issuance of these\nwaivers will be published in order to provide better transparency and\naccountability.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2017-18: S.3808 - Referred to Finance\n\n2019-20: S.2179 - Referred to Finance\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the sixtieth day after it shall have\nbecome a law provided, however, that:\na. The amendments to subdivisions 6 of section 313 of the executive law\nmade by section one of this act shall not affect the expiration of arti-\ncle 15-A of such law and shall be deemed expired. therewith;\nb. The amendments made to subdivision 3 of section 311 of the executive\nJaw by section two of this act shall not affect the expiration of arti-\ncle 15-A of such law and shall be deemed expired therewith; and\nc. The director of the division of minority and women's business devel-\nopment and the commissioner of labor shall immediately promulgate rules\nand regulations and take all other necessary actions to implement the\nprovisions of this act on or before such effective date\n",
